※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A variable at the top level may hold a record.
※
※ **Its value is bytes in the image**, laid out by the one thing that says where
※ a field goes, so what the image holds and what the program reads out of it
※ cannot drift.  Every field is given and each once, which is the rule a record
※ written anywhere follows; padding between fields is zeroes, which a program
※ cannot read and so cannot tell from anything.
※
※ **It is a place for as long as the program is**, so a field of it is read at an
※ offset, written at one, and `&moving.to.x` is an address like any other -- the
※ same three things a record local got, reached the same way from here down.
※
※ **A record without `mut` is a record that cannot be written**, field or no
※ field, which is the rule every other variable follows.
※
※ Compare: **C**, whose static initializer this is, down to the zero padding;
※ **Rust**, where a `static` wants a `const` expression and this is one;
※ **Go**, whose package-level variable is initialized by generated code where
※ the value is not constant, which is the other answer; **Zig**, whose
※ `comptime` evaluation makes far more than this constant.

type Point = x : u32 ; y : u32
type Line = from : Point ; to : Point

let origin: Line = Line(.from ← Point(.x ← 1u32, .y ← 2u32),
                        .to ← Point(.x ← 3u32, .y ← 4u32))
let moving: mut Line = Line(.from ← Point(.x ← 0u32, .y ← 0u32),
                            .to ← Point(.x ← 0u32, .y ← 0u32))

@[impure]
fn bump(at: &mut u32):
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + 1u32

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    moving.to.x ← 5u32
    ※ a whole nested field, copied out of the one that cannot be written
    moving.from ← origin.from
    ※ and a reference into it
    bump(&moving.to.x)
    if origin.to.y ≠ 4u32:
        1u6
    elif moving.to.x ≠ 6u32:
        2u6
    elif moving.from.x ≠ 1u32 ∨ moving.from.y ≠ 2u32:
        3u6
    ※ what nothing wrote is what it started as
    elif moving.to.y ≠ 0u32:
        4u6
    else:
        0u6
